      {
        "introduction": "As a lasting reminder of how the wicked are brought down, Ezekiel writes a lament for two of Judah's kings who were taken by Judah's oppressors. It has been some 17 years since Pharaoh Necho led King Jehoahaz away to captivity in Egypt, and some six years since Nebuchadnezzar brought a subdued King Jehoiakin to Babylonia. Their capture and exile are witness to Judah's vulnerability to God's judgment."
      },
      {
        "title": "",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "title": "Lament for the Kings",
            "references": [
              {
                "book": "Ezekiel",
                "chapter": 19,
                "verse": "1-9"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "introduction": "In the following year, 590 B.C., God once again chastises Israel, citing their long history of rebellion and its repeated reluctance to bring ultimate punishment against them. He reminds Israel that they continue to be as sinful as their fathers, but that the day is coming when God's chosen ones will be an obedient and holy people."
      },
